F-5 — The same child, not caught
  [At step] S-4, which then goes on at S-6.
  [What sets it off] a child the registry already holds as a learner is entered with a family name, given names or date of birth that differs from that learner's in anything but capitals — a family name that differs by one letter among them. The search finds none.
  [What the clerk sees] SC-3, the screen of a new learner: that a new learner number was given, and that number.
  [What is saved] a second record for the same child, with a new learner number (S-6), so that the registry registers the same child twice. This is a limit of the search step, the brief's own rule: of the data's 8 children on more than one list, the search catches 4.
    <brief:7> 7: Progressa's Ministry of Education, Youth and Skills (MoEYS) wants one list of learners instead of three. Before anyone builds the real thing, the planning unit wants to see a small working prototype: a school clerk enrols a child, and the registry does not register the same child twice. The prototype is for agreement. It is not the registry.
    <brief:13> 13: - if it finds exactly one learner with the same family name, given names and date of birth (capitals ignored), it shows that learner and enters nothing new;
    <brief:14> 14: - if it finds none, it gives the child a new learner number and saves the record;
    <R-01> 17: **Cites:** U-03, brief line 7
    <R-01> 19: > Before anyone builds the real thing, the planning unit wants to see a small working prototype: a school clerk enrols a child, and the registry does not register the same child twice.
    <R-01> 21: **Requirement.** The prototype is a small working prototype in which a school clerk enrols a child and the registry does not register the same child twice.
    <R-01> 23: **Test.**
